# MathScan: Project Summary

## What it is

MathScan is a web application that converts photographed or scanned handwritten university course notes into editable LaTeX and plain text, with an export path to a typeset PDF. The target user is a student who wants to digitize handwritten math, economics, or general coursework notes without manually retyping them. The project was built under a self-imposed deadline (late July 2026) and a hard constraint: no paid APIs or paid models anywhere in the pipeline. The primary motivation stated throughout development was portfolio and resume value ahead of internship applications, not commercial launch, which shaped several deliberate scope and quality tradeoffs described below.

## Architecture and technology choices

The backend is a FastAPI application written in Python, deployed as a container on Modal. Modal was chosen over a traditional VM (Hetzner) or RunPod's serverless model because it can host the existing FastAPI app's Server-Sent-Events streaming endpoints almost unchanged via `@modal.asgi_app()`, whereas RunPod's single-job-handler model would have required re-architecting the streaming endpoints from scratch. The deployment runs CPU-only rather than on a GPU: an initial GPU deploy attempt failed because Pix2Text's ONNX runtime requires a CUDA-specific package not present in the container, and CPU inference is both a known-working configuration (matching local development, which also has no GPU) and cheaper per second of Modal billing. Containers scale to zero after five idle minutes with no traffic.

The frontend is a Next.js 15 / React 19 application styled with Tailwind, using KaTeX for live LaTeX rendering, deployed on Vercel. State such as conversion history and share links intentionally avoids a full account system: history lives in the browser's local storage, and only share links (which by definition need to be opened by someone other than the person who ran the conversion) are persisted server-side, via Modal's key-value dictionary storage with an in-memory fallback for local testing.

For document export, the backend uses Tectonic, a single self-contained LaTeX binary, rather than a full TeX Live installation, to keep the container image small. Getting Tectonic running inside the Modal container required working around several real, non-obvious dependency issues: missing OpenCV shared libraries (`libgl1`, `libglib2.0-0`) needed transitively by Pix2Text's layout module, missing text-shaping libraries (`libgraphite2-3`, `libharfbuzz0b`, `libicu-dev`) needed by Tectonic itself, and a glibc version mismatch that was resolved by switching to Tectonic's musl-linked "semistatic" release build instead of the standard glibc-linked one.

## The OCR and AI pipeline

The core recognition model is Pix2Text, a math-formula-aware OCR/layout model that was chosen specifically because it natively understands mathematical notation and outputs real LaTeX, unlike a general OCR engine. Development found early on that Pix2Text's full-page layout-detection API misclassified real handwritten math as a "figure" and skipped it entirely; switching to its lower-level `recognize_text_formula` method, which treats the whole image as text-and-formula content without a layout-detection pass first, fixed this immediately.

Because Pix2Text is specialized for math notation, it performs measurably worse on dense handwritten prose (history, political science, or economics notes that are mostly sentences rather than equations). To address this, PaddleOCR, a general-purpose OCR engine, runs as a second opinion. This fallback evolved twice during the project. Initially it was gated behind Pix2Text's own self-reported confidence score, only running when that score fell below a threshold. Real testing later found a page where Pix2Text reported roughly 85% confidence while actually producing hallucinated, largely nonsensical output (stray Chinese characters, jumbled letter fragments) — a case where the model was confidently wrong, and the confidence-gated fallback never triggered on exactly the page that needed it. A cheaper proposed fix (a dictionary-based "fraction of real English words" heuristic) was tested against real data before being written into the app and found to perform backwards — it flagged a genuinely good calculus page as more suspicious than the actual hallucinated prose page, because isolated math fragments like "sqrt" aren't English dictionary words. The fix that was actually shipped removes the gate entirely: PaddleOCR now always runs as a second opinion whenever the fallback is enabled, and the two results are compared directly, with whichever one scores higher confidence being kept. This is an explicit, accepted cost/latency tradeoff — every request now pays for both models' compute time, not just the ones that looked uncertain.

A separate free-tier LLM correction pass runs after OCR, using Groq's hosted Llama 3.3 70B model (chosen because it is a free, fast-inference endpoint sufficient for light text cleanup, not because it was the most capable model available). This pass runs once per page on the full combined page text, rather than per line or per region, specifically so the model has whole-page context to catch a misread word that doesn't fit the surrounding sentence. It is a genuine no-op with no API key configured, which is how the automated test suite runs it.

The correction pass is deliberately guarded against hallucination, and the guard's design went through several real iterations driven by actual failures, not speculation. An early version rejected a page's correction if the total word-count drift exceeded a percentage threshold; this missed a real incident where the model inserted three entirely new words into an otherwise-correct sentence, because the insertion was a small enough fraction of a long page to stay under the threshold. The guard was then tightened to reject any word inserted or deleted at all, which fixed that case but proved too strict in the other direction: it also blocked legitimate fixes that needed a word removed, such as raw OCR text reading "lecture chopper 2" where the handwriting actually said "Chapter 2," which requires both a word swap and dropping a stray word. Following an explicit product decision to prioritize actually fixing errors over guaranteeing zero risk of an inserted word, the guard was loosened to a whole-page character-similarity floor. That version, in turn, was found to have its own gap: a single badly garbled line was fully replaced with a fluent but entirely wrong invented sentence, and because the rest of a long page was untouched, the page-wide similarity ratio barely moved. The current version operates per contiguous edited chunk rather than per word or per whole page: small edits (five words or fewer) are always allowed, since those represent ordinary grammar or misread fixes, but a larger changed chunk must still meaningfully resemble the original text it replaces. Each revision of this guard was calibrated against real, previously observed failures before being shipped, and the file's own comments preserve this history rather than presenting the current version as the only one ever considered.

Reading order and page assembly also went through a documented false start. An early implementation ordered recognized regions using Pix2Text's own `line_number` field, which appeared to work in testing but turned out to come back empty (`None`) on real handwritten pages, meaning the fix silently never engaged in production. This was only discovered by directly inspecting real benchmark output and noticing the exact same "one word per blank line" fragmentation pattern the fix was supposed to eliminate. The replacement approach derives reading order purely from each region's bounding-box geometry: regions are grouped into visual lines by vertical-center proximity relative to the page's median line height, then ordered left-to-right within a line and top-to-bottom across lines. This was verified against real Pix2Text output (confirming bounding boxes are reliably populated, unlike `line_number`) and confirmed working correctly in a live exported PDF.

A related, separate bug was found in PDF export itself: Tectonic aborts compilation of the entire document on the first fatal LaTeX error, so a single garbled OCR region containing invalid LaTeX (for example, a text-mode-only command used inside math mode) caused the whole multi-page export to fail, even though every other page was fine. The fix makes export resilient rather than all-or-nothing: on a compile failure, the specific offending line reported by Tectonic's own error output is commented out and compilation is retried, up to five times, before falling back to the original hard failure with full context if no progress can be made.

## Evaluation methodology

An accuracy benchmark harness runs the real production recognition pipeline (including the PaddleOCR fallback and Groq correction, matching what live users actually get) against a hand-labeled test set, computing two complementary metrics: character-level accuracy via edit distance, and a second, order-insensitive word-overlap recall metric. The two metrics were tracked separately because edit distance alone punishes reading-order problems as harshly as genuinely wrong content, which made an early version of the reading-order bug hard to diagnose from the aggregate number alone. The test set grew over the project from 3 to 16 real images, hand-transcribed directly from the user's own uploaded course PDFs (calculus, economics, and political science notes), with some candidate pages deliberately excluded (true radial mind-maps with no defensible single reading order, or mostly blank pages).

The benchmark's own execution had two notable real bugs of its own. Running many images through one long-lived process caused a silent, untraceable crash after a couple of successful images, traced to native-code (Paddle/ONNX) resource buildup across repeated in-process model loads; the fix isolates each image into its own subprocess. Separately, running many images in parallel initially caused every image to time out, because several subprocesses each loading a full copy of the recognition model simultaneously contend for the same CPU cores; sequential execution with a longer per-image timeout was needed once the fallback model started running on every image rather than only some.

## Honest results and known limitations

After all of the fixes described above, a full benchmark run across the 16-image test set produced 33.0% mean character accuracy and 50.5% mean word-overlap recall, against a stated target of 80% character accuracy. This is essentially unchanged from the pre-fix baseline of 32.2% and 52.2%. This is treated in the project's own records as an honest, expected result rather than a failure of the fixes: the fixes were real, individually verified bugs that improved correctness, safety, and user-facing behavior (confirmed directly against actual live exports, not assumed), but the aggregate character-accuracy metric is dominated by the underlying recognition models' genuine ceiling on messy handwriting, particularly on notation with less common symbols such as set-theory notation (intersection, set-builder syntax, the "not an element of" symbol), which both a per-region confidence gate and reading-order logic cannot address. Reaching materially higher accuracy from this point would require either a paid, more capable model or fine-tuning a custom model, both explicitly out of scope given the project's free-tier constraint and fixed deadline.

## Current state and remaining options

As of the most recent work, the backend and frontend are both deployed (Modal and Vercel respectively), continuous integration runs the automated test suite on every push via GitHub Actions, and the repository's git history and in-code comments document each real bug found and its fix, including cases where an earlier fix was later found not to work and was replaced. Remaining, optional, lower-urgency work identified but not yet done includes growing the test set further toward 30-50 images for a more statistically defensible accuracy claim, and writing a consolidated README with the architecture and these findings for anyone evaluating the project directly. Both were assessed as lower-value than the debugging work already completed, given the fixed deadline and the free-tier accuracy ceiling already reached.
